{"chunk_id":"2a43a5","wall_time_seconds":0.0000025,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":3545,"output":"{"baseRefName": "main", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/54\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## What breaks\n\n`TreeBasePath` shards a commit target on its own first two hex characters (`t.Value[:2]`). `ParseTarget` enforced a 3-character minimum \u2014 but nothing else did, and the `Target` `materialize.go` rebuilds from a tree leaf path never goes through `ParseTarget`.\n\nA metadata tree carrying `commit/ab/a/k/__value` materialized into a `State` with a one-character commit value, and the very next write re-serialized it:\n\n```\npanic: runtime error: slice bounds out of range [:2] with length 1\n    gitmeta.TreeBasePath      treepath.go:40\n    gitmeta.buildKeyTreePath\n    gitmeta.TreePath\n    gitmeta.Serialize         <- MetaStore.updateLocked, every write\n```\n\nThat is the whole read-materialize-CAS loop `MetaStore` runs, so one such record crashes the process on every subsequent pass. The store is local today; once git-meta records are exchanged over a shared ref (P1), the tree is bytes another member wrote.\n\n## The fix\n\nThree changes, at the three places that can stop it:\n\n- **`ValidateTargetValue` enforces `MinTargetValueLen`**, the minimum `ParseTarget` has always documented. `Serialize` is the only gate between a materialized `State` and the tree, so the rule belongs where every write path passes through it. `ParseTarget` now delegates rather than keeping a second copy.\n- **`parseLeafPath` validates the target it reconstructs**, so a hostile tree is classified as `ErrMalformedTree` at the READ boundary \u2014 the permanent failure a consumer is already documented to drop \u2014 instead of being carried into `State`.\n- **`TreeBasePath` shards through `shardPrefix`.** It returns no error, so a caller that somehow reaches it unvalidated must get a wrong-but-total path rather than a panic.\n\n## Blast radius\n\nNothing in this repo builds a branch or change-id target, and the only commit target is `entityindex.commitTarget(sha)` with a 40-hex SHA, so the minimum cannot regress real usage.\n\n## How it was found\n\n`FuzzTreePath`, from the fuzzing campaign. The minimized inputs ship as seed corpus entries for `FuzzTreePath` and `FuzzMaterializeRoundTrip` (inert until those harnesses land in the fuzz-targets PR). `TestShortTargetValueIsRejectedNotPanic` and `TestMaterializeRejectsShortTargetValue` are the standalone regressions; both fail on the pre-fix code.\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...`, `go vet ./internal/...`, `gofmt -l`, and `go test ./internal/factgitmeta/... ./internal/entityindex/...` \u2014 all green. Post-fix, `FuzzTreePath` ran 16.5M execs and `FuzzMaterializeRoundTrip` 7.5M execs with no crasher. The full suite was not run on this host: it is under heavy load from a parallel audit fleet and long runs are timing out environmentally.", "headRefName": "audit/gitmeta-shard-panic", "headRefOid": "84173018bb1b67c02c33ffeb510fc9a9e52403dc", "number": 126, "state": "OPEN", "title": "fix(gitmeta): reject a target value too short to shard instead of panicking", "url": "https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/126"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/gitmeta-shard-panic", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/55\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## What breaks\n\n`ValidateTargetValue` returned early for path targets, so **no** rule applied to them. Path values legitimately contain `/` \u2014 they are stored as nested, escaped segments under the `__target__` sentinel \u2014 but that exemption also skipped every segment-level check the format needs.\n\nA value with an empty segment (`\"/\"`, `\"a//b\"`, a leading or trailing slash) encodes to a tree path with an empty component:\n\n```\npath:\"/\"  key:\"0\"   ->   \"path///__target__/0/__value\"\n```\n\n`BuildTree` then refuses that path \u2014 and it refuses the **whole `Serialize` call**, not just the offending record. One such record therefore makes the entire store unwritable for every writer that materializes it, with no repair path from the reading side.\n\n## The fix\n\n`ValidatePathTargetValue` restores the segment rules: no empty segment, no `.` or `..` segment, no NUL. Values that legitimately contain slashes are unaffected (`src/main.rs`, `a/b/c`, `~weird`, `__reserved/x` all still serialize).\n\n`parseLeafPath` validates the path target it reconstructs from a tree, for the same reason the other target types do: on a shared ref the tree is untrusted input, and a value the serializer could not have produced belongs to `ErrMalformedTree` at the read boundary.\n\nThe `entire-api` copy of this vendored engine already carries a check of this name; this copy had lost it \u2014 the divergence note at the top of the file already flagged exactly this.\n\n## How it was found\n\n`FuzzTreePath`, from the fuzzing campaign, after 7.4M execs: `encoded path \"path///__target__/0/__value\" has an empty component (type=\"path\" value=\"/\" key=\"0\")`. The minimized input ships as a seed corpus entry (inert until that harness lands in the fuzz-targets PR).\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...`, `go vet ./internal/...`, `gofmt -l`, and `go test ./internal/factgitmeta/...` \u2014 all green. The full suite was not run on this host: it is under heavy load from a parallel audit fleet and long runs are timing out environmentally.\n\n## Note\n\nTouches the same two files as #126 (a separate defect: a commit target value too short to shard, which panics). Both are independently valid off `main`; whichever lands second will need a small conflict resolution in `ValidateTargetValue` and `parseLeafPath`.", "headRefName": "audit/fuzz-path-target-validation", "headRefOid": "2334f0415d4f2cb657cdd2e00af7dc74e46540e4", "number": 127, "state": "OPEN", "title": "fix(gitmeta): validate path-target values instead of exempting them", "url": "https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/127"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/fuzz-path-target-validation", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/56\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## What breaks\n\n`ParseNDJSON` scanned with a 64 KiB line bound. `WriteNDJSON` enforced nothing. A fact whose serialized record exceeded that bound wrote cleanly and then failed to load:\n\n```\nbufio.Scanner: token too long\n```\n\nSo the store could persist a fact-set it could not read back \u2014 with no position and no bound in the error to act on.\n\nOn the shared cross-member head this is worse than local corruption. `factsync.Sync` parses the pulled blob with `ParseNDJSON`, so **one** member's oversized fact permanently fails **every other** member's sync, and the affected members cannot repair it, because the fact only exists on the head they cannot read.\n\n## The fix\n\n- `MaxLineBytes` is **16 MiB**, matching the ceiling the rest of the brain applies to untrusted inputs it reads whole (`safe_read.go`).\n- `WriteNDJSON` refuses a record whose line would exceed it (`ErrRecordTooLarge`), so the two halves can never disagree again. Nothing is written when it refuses.\n- An oversized line already on disk is reported as a `*ParseError` naming the line number and the bound, not as an opaque `bufio` error.\n\n## How it was found\n\n`FuzzNDJSONWriteReadRoundTrip`, which asserts the property directly: any record the store is willing to write, the reader must be able to read back. It failed on the first non-trivial size. The minimized input ships as a seed corpus entry (inert until that harness lands in the fuzz-targets PR).\n\n`TestWriteReadRoundTripAtRealisticSizes`, `TestWriteRefusesUnreadableRecord`, and `TestParseReportsOversizedLineWithPosition` are the standalone regressions.\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...`, `go vet ./internal/...`, `gofmt -l`, and `go test ./internal/factmerge/ ./internal/factsync/` \u2014 all green. The full suite was not run on this host: it is under heavy load from a parallel audit fleet and long runs are timing out environmentally.", "headRefName": "audit/ndjson-line-cap", "headRefOid": "9b2ab9eceadc5605507943f886cd8c75cb2d5f9d", "number": 128, "state": "OPEN", "title": "fix(factmerge): stop the fact-set writer emitting lines its own reader rejects", "url": "https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/128"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/ndjson-line-cap", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/57\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## What breaks\n\nCanonical form passes unknown members through on purpose (ADR 0001, additive-only: an older verifier must be able to canonicalize and verify a **newer** producer's artifact). \"Unknown\" was decided by **exact** match against the contract keys \u2014 but Go's `encoding/json` matches object members to struct fields **case-insensitively**.\n\nSo a member like `manIfest` is unknown to this canonicalizer and **is** the manifest to `encoding/json`. The same signed bytes then carry two different meanings depending on which decoder reads them:\n\n```go\nraw := `{\"manIfest\":{\"repo_key\":\"EVIL\",\"generated_at\":\"2026-01-02T03:04:05Z\",\"brain_schema_version\":\"1.0\"}}`\n\njson.Unmarshal(raw, &BrainArtifact{})   // RepoKey == \"EVIL\"\nCanonicalUnmarshal(raw, &artifact)      // RepoKey == \"\"      (zero-filled)\n```\n\nand `VerifyBytes` accepts the bytes either way, because canonicalization preserves the unknown key verbatim. One signature, two readings, decided by the consumer's parser rather than by the signer.\n\nThis is the same class of cross-parser differential `canonical.go` already rejects duplicate members for, and it is not hypothetical: the artifact is consumed across a repo boundary (`entire-api`) by a Go decoder this repo does not control.\n\n## The fix\n\n`normalizeObject` rejects an unknown member that case-folds to a contract field. Every contract key is lowercase ASCII, so no legitimate artifact carries one. Genuinely unknown newer-minor fields still pass through untouched, and canonical form is still a fixed point over them \u2014 `TestUnknownFieldsStillPassThrough` pins both. Unknown keys are visited in sorted order so input with several collisions produces a deterministic error.\n\n`strings.EqualFold` is a superset of `encoding/json`'s own fold matching for ASCII contract keys, so nothing the decoder would fold slips past.\n\n## How it was found\n\n`FuzzUnknownFieldsCannotSmuggle`, which states the ADR 0001 obligation as a testable property: for any two byte strings that verify under the same signature, the decoded `BrainArtifact` must be identical. It failed in ~3 s. The minimized input ships as a seed corpus entry (inert until that harness lands in the fuzz-targets PR).\n\n`TestCanonicalizeRejectsCaseFoldedContractKeys` and `TestSignedArtifactCannotBeReinterpretedByFolding` are the standalone regressions; the latter reproduces the full smuggling scenario end to end.\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...`, `go vet ./internal/...`, `gofmt -l`, and `go test ./internal/brainwire/` (173 tests) \u2014 all green. Post-fix, `FuzzUnknownFieldsCannotSmuggle` ran 11.0M execs and `FuzzVerifyBytesRejectsTamper` 19.8M execs with no crasher. The full suite was not run on this host: it is under heavy load from a parallel audit fleet and long runs are timing out environmentally.", "headRefName": "audit/canonical-case-fold", "headRefOid": "918854e8aa17269bdff60d7423a300a835b04d64", "number": 129, "state": "OPEN", "title": "fix(brainwire): reject case-folded contract keys in canonical form", "url": "https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/129"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/canonical-case-fold", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/68\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n**Two things: permanent fuzz infrastructure, plus one new bug it found.**\n\n### The targets\nThe campaign's crashers were each fixed on their own branch (#126, #127, #128, #129) but the *targets that found them* were not kept, so nothing stops the same classes returning. This adds them as the permanent regression surface, each with its minimized seed corpus: canonical encoding + signature verification (`FuzzVerifyBytesRejectsTamper`, `FuzzUnknownFieldsCannotSmuggle`, `FuzzSignatureEnvelope`, `FuzzCheckCompatibility`), the fact-set NDJSON round trip, git-meta tree paths and target sharding, the hosted client's response handling, the entity-index document, and agent transcript scanning (`FuzzScanConversationTranscript`, `FuzzDistillDocumentConversation`, \u2026).\n\n**This branch is stacked on #126\u2013#129** \u2014 those fixes are merged in, so the targets run green here. On bare main two of them correctly fail, which is the point.\n\n### The new finding\nRunning them turned up one more, fixed in this commit: **`encoding/json` does not fail on invalid UTF-8, it substitutes U+FFFD.** A record written with such a byte **read back changed, with no error on either side** \u2014 silent corruption. Fact paths come from real filenames and POSIX permits arbitrary bytes there, so an ordinary repository reaches this; on the shared cross-member head the mangled path would then merge into every other member.\n\n`WriteNDJSON` now refuses such a record with `ErrInvalidUTF8`, naming the offending field, on exactly the rule already established by `ErrRecordTooLarge`: never persist what this package cannot faithfully return.\n\n**Tests:** refusal across paths/text/locus/provenance, each asserting the field is named and nothing was written; plus `TestWriteNDJSONAcceptsValidUTF8` so the guard cannot start rejecting legitimate non-ASCII (`caf\u00e9/\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e.go`, `na\u00efve \u2014 \u00fcn\u00efcode \u2713`).\n\n**Mutation-verified:** disabling the guard reproduces the original fuzz failure \u2014 `Paths:[]string{\"\\x85\"}` written, `Paths:[]string{\"\ufffd\"}` read back.", "headRefName": "audit/fuzz-targets", "headRefOid": "db6a7ef60f809bcfa601710e26f89d360902fa52", "number": 140, "state": "OPEN", "title": "test(fuzz): keep the fuzz targets, and refuse records JSON would mangle", "url": "https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/140"}
"}

