{"chunk_id":"395b58","wall_time_seconds":0.000001709,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":4987,"output":"{"baseRefName": "main", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/45\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## Impact\n\n`mcpPositiveInt` and `mcpNonNegativeInt` (`internal/cli/mcp.go:1378`, `:1396`) enforced a floor and nothing else \u2014 `typed <= float64(math.MaxInt)` is not a bound, it is a type check.\n\nAn MCP client is not a trusted peer in this design: the agent driving it carries repository text in its context that an untrusted checkout can influence, and a `tools/call` is one line of JSON.\n\nThe argument is not merely a result count. It reaches allocation sizing:\n\n- `internal/cli/history_fts.go:581` \u2014 `out := make([]scoredHistoryRecord, 0, limit)`\n- `internal/cli/doc_fts.go:199,204` \u2014 `LIMIT ?` with `limit*4`, and `make([]scoredDocRecord, 0, limit*4)`\n- `internal/cli/retrieve.go:209,322` \u2014 the history arm scales the same value by 2 then 3 (with overflow guards, but no ceiling)\n\nSo `{\"name\":\"brain_search\",\"arguments\":{\"query\":\"x\",\"limit\":1000000000}}` decides how many elements the server asks the runtime for. Depending on element size that is either a panic the request recovery turns into an opaque `-32603 internal error` (what I observed end to end), or an allocation request under the `makeslice` cap that the process attempts and does not survive. Either way an unauthenticated argument, not the server, picks the number.\n\n`context_lines` on `brain_get_code_snippet` has the same shape \u2014 `semantic_graph.go:894` adds it to `record.EndLine`, and `math.MaxInt64` there overflows the sum negative.\n\n## Repro\n\n`internal/cli/mcp_int_bounds_test.go`. Before the fix:\n\n```\n--- FAIL: TestMCPIntegerArgumentsAreCapped\n    mcpPositiveInt accepted limit=1e+09; it sizes a slice capacity\n    mcpNonNegativeInt accepted limit=1e+09\n    mcpPositiveInt accepted limit=1.099511627776e+12; it sizes a slice capacity\n    mcpPositiveInt accepted limit=2147483647; it sizes a slice capacity\n--- FAIL: TestMCPToolCallRejectsOversizedLimit\n    error = {\"code\":-32603,\"message\":\"internal error handling \\\"tools/call\\\"\"}\n```\n\nThat last line is the whole point: on `main` an oversized `limit` does not produce a validation error, it produces a recovered internal failure. After the fix it is a clean `-32000 limit must be an integer between 1 and 10000`.\n\n## Fix\n\n`mcpIntegerArgMax = 10000`, enforced in both decoders. Far above any useful result count, depth, offset or context window; far below the point where the value becomes an allocation problem.\n\nEnforced in the decoders rather than declared as `\"maximum\"` in each `inputSchema` **on purpose**: the tool definitions are sent on every `tools/list` and their size is budgeted in tokens \u2014 `TestMCPBrainBriefToolDefinitionGolden` asserts an exact 747-byte definition and documents the tokenizer accounting for the whole `tools/list` payload. Adding a `maximum` to ~25 integer properties would spend that budget restating a bound the server must check itself regardless. `brain_get_conversation` and `brain_concepts`, which already declare real semantic maxima, keep them.\n\nTwo existing assertions in `mcp_test.go` move from `\"limit must be an integer greater than zero\"` to `\"limit must be an integer between 1 and\"`. The rejection behaviour they cover is unchanged; only the message is more accurate.\n\n## Mutation\n\nRestoring `math.MaxInt` in either decoder reproduces the failures above.\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (full suite, exit 0), `gofmt -l internal/` clean, `go vet ./internal/cli/` clean.", "headRefName": "audit/sec-mcp-int-bounds", "number": 117, "title": "fix(mcp): integer tool arguments have no upper bound, so a tool call chooses an allocation size"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/sec-mcp-int-bounds", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/61\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n**Severity: MED (confused deputy, irreversible).** `brain_delete_project`, `brain_list_projects` and the workspace fan-out tools reached **any repo on the machine** by `repo_key`, with no scope check. The MCP surface is driven by an agent whose context can be poisoned by hostile repository content, so a tool call made while working in repo A could enumerate or **irreversibly delete** repo B's brain data.\n\n**Fix:** these tools now stay inside the repository the MCP server was bound to, with one refusal message that always names the single gate (`ENTIRE_BRAIN_MCP_ALLOW_CROSS_REPO`) so an operator who genuinely wants cross-repo access knows the knob.\n\n**Deliberately NOT changed:** the plain CLI stays cross-repo \u2014 a human at a terminal is not the confused deputy. `TestWorkspaceCLIStaysCrossRepoWithoutGate` pins that, so this fix cannot silently break the legitimate workspace workflow.\n\n**Tests (8):** foreign repo_key refused / bound key allowed / gate honored, for delete-project, list-projects and the workspace tools, plus the CLI-unaffected guarantee. Tool descriptions and docs updated to state the scoping.\n\n**Mutation-verified:** forcing `mcpCrossRepoAllowed()` to `true` fails all three refusal tests.\n\n**Verification:** `go build ./...` clean; targeted cross-repo/scope tests pass. Full suite not re-run on a loaded host.", "headRefName": "audit/sec-cross-repo-scope", "number": 133, "title": "fix(mcp): scope project and workspace tools to the bound repository"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/sec-cross-repo-scope", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/46\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## Summary\n\nContract audit of the entire-brain MCP tool surface (all 36 tools registered in `internal/cli/mcp.go`), comparing each tool's declared name/description/JSON-schema against what its handler actually reads and does, and cross-referencing `README.md` and `docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md`. Full drift table below. This surface is about to be wrapped by a separate P2 unified-MCP project, so drift here becomes a foundation bug that propagates.\n\nThree real drift items found and fixed (smallest complete fix on the side judged wrong \u2014 see notes):\n\n- **`brain_search_graph`'s `offset` and `brain_get_code_snippet`'s `context_lines`** declared `minimum: 1` in their JSON schema, but both handlers (`mcpNonNegativeInt`) accept and default to `0`. A schema-validating MCP client would wrongly refuse to send the default value. Fixed the schema (added a `nonNegativeIntegerArg` helper, `minimum: 0`) since the handler behavior is correct and long-standing; the declared contract was stale.\n- **`brain_get_architecture`'s description** claimed \"boundary counts\", a field `semanticGraphSchemaReport`/`graphMetrics` has never returned. It dispatches to the exact same handler as `brain_get_graph_schema` (`runSemanticGraphSchema`), whose real metrics are hotspots, entry points, packages, and layers. Fixed the description to match actual output and made the alias explicit, consistent with how the other aliased tools (`brain_index_status`, `brain_search_code`, `brain_detect_changes`) document their aliasing.\n- **`docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md`'s tool catalog** was missing `brain_entity_history` \u2014 registered in code and documented in `README.md`, but absent from the guide's \"Available tools\" list.\n\nAdded regression tests for each: schema-minimum lock for the two zero-accepting params, a description lock for `brain_get_architecture`, and a doc-contract test that every registered MCP tool name appears in the guide (would have caught the `brain_entity_history` gap).\n\n## Drift table (all 36 registered tools)\n\n| Tool | Drift | Severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| brain_status | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_refresh | none (worktree/semantic/force/skipSessions/60s cap all match) | \u2014 |\n| brain_brief | none (packet_format enum matches handler) | \u2014 |\n| brain_query | none (retrieval schema enforcement verified in `buildRetrievalOptions`/`retrieveUnifiedWithOptions`) | \u2014 |\n| brain_search | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_vsearch | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_get | none (context/outline params match `conversationContextMax`/`conversationOutlineMaxLimit` constants) | \u2014 |\n| brain_multi_get | none (`maxItems` matches `maxGetBatchIDs`) | \u2014 |\n| brain_context | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_impact | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_changes | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_code | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_index_status | none (verified true alias of brain_status) | \u2014 |\n| brain_index_repository | minor: top-level description says \"Build or refresh\" but default (`force=false`) errors if an index exists; fully clarified by the `force` param's own description (\"Replace... even when one exists\") | doc-only (not fixed \u2014 already disambiguated) |\n| brain_list_projects | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_delete_project | none (confirm-required guard matches) | \u2014 |\n| brain_search_code | none (verified true alias of brain_code) | \u2014 |\n| brain_search_graph | **FIXED**: schema `minimum:1` on `offset` contradicted handler accepting/defaulting to 0 | behavior-diverges-from-doc |\n| brain_query_graph | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_get_graph_schema | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_get_code_snippet | **FIXED**: schema `minimum:1` on `context_lines` contradicted handler accepting/defaulting to 0 | behavior-diverges-from-doc |\n| brain_trace_path | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_dead_code | none (query logic matches \"no incoming non-structural edges and no handler boundary\" exactly) | \u2014 |\n| brain_detect_changes | none (verified true alias of brain_changes) | \u2014 |\n| brain_get_architecture | **FIXED**: description claimed \"boundary counts\", a field the shared handler's report never returns | behavior-diverges-from-doc |\n| brain_ingest_traces | none (Matched/Unmatched static-edge fields confirm \"validate dynamic edges against the static graph\") | \u2014 |\n| brain_tests | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_boundaries | minor: `inspectBoundarySpec`'s invalid-kind error text says `--kind` (CLI flag syntax) even when reached via the MCP JSON param `kind`; cosmetic, out of scope for this pass | doc-only (flagged, not fixed) |\n| brain_regressions | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_review | none (schema_version contract confirmed present, golden test already covers this) | \u2014 |\n| brain_workspace_regressions | none | \u2014 |\n| brain_workspace_graph | none (Contracts/cross_edges fields confirmed) | \u2014 |\n| brain_workspace_review | none (golden test already covers this) | \u2014 |\n| brain_patterns | none (type/scope are plain filters, no enum enforced \u2014 matches description's informal style used elsewhere, e.g. `profile`) | \u2014 |\n| brain_entity_history | **FIXED (doc)**: missing from `docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md`'s tool catalog (present in README.md and code) | doc-only |\n| brain_patterns_status | none | \u2014 |\n\n## Out of scope (flagged, not touched)\n\n- `brain_boundaries`'s CLI-flag-flavored error text (`--kind must be route, tool, or workflow`) leaking into the MCP error path \u2014 cosmetic, shared with the CLI command, not a contract-shape bug.\n- `brain_index_repository`'s top-level description wording (\"Build or refresh\") vs. the force-required-to-replace default \u2014 already fully disambiguated by the `force` parameter's own description text, so left as-is to avoid unnecessary churn.\n\nNeither affects correctness of the declared JSON schema or handler behavior; both are follow-up polish for a separate pass.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `go build ./...`\n- [x] `go vet ./...`\n- [x] `go test ./...` (2700 tests, all packages green)\n- [x] New tests added: `TestMCPZeroValuedIntegerArgsDeclareNonNegativeMinimum`, `TestMCPBrainGetArchitectureDescriptionMatchesGraphSchemaOutput`, and a doc-contract catalog-completeness check in `TestDocContractCapabilityMatrix`", "headRefName": "audit/contract-mcp", "number": 118, "title": "fix(mcp): correct MCP tool schema/description drift from handler behavior"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/contract-mcp", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/77\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nContract audit of the MCP tool surface, pass 2. #118 compared each tool's declared schema against its handler and fixed three items; this is the adversarial re-check of the rows it marked \"none\", plus the drift #117 introduced.\n\n## The drift\n\n`mcpPositiveInt`/`mcpNonNegativeInt` reject any integer argument above `mcpIntegerArgMax` (10000) with a `-32000` tool error. The JSON schema declared a floor and **no ceiling** on 27 of the surface's 30 integer arguments.\n\nSo a schema-validating MCP client believed `{\"name\":\"brain_search\",\"arguments\":{\"query\":\"x\",\"limit\":1000000}}` was a legal call, sent it, and learned the real bound by being refused at runtime. The declaration understated the contract the handler enforces. #117 added the cap; the schema was never told about it.\n\n`brain_get` was the exception \u2014 its `context_before`/`context_after`/`limit` already declared the stricter ceilings their downstream validator enforces (`conversationContextMax`, `conversationOutlineMaxLimit`). Its `after_turn` did not, and is fixed here.\n\n## Fix\n\n`maximum` declared alongside `minimum` in the `integerArg`/`nonNegativeIntegerArg` helpers and on `brain_get`'s inline `after_turn` schema. No handler behaviour changes \u2014 this makes the declaration state what the handler already did.\n\n## Tests that pin the two sides together\n\n- **`TestMCPIntegerArgsDeclareTheCeilingTheHandlerEnforces`** walks every integer argument on every tool and requires a declared `maximum` that is never above what the handler accepts. It proves the generic ceiling by calling `mcpPositiveInt`/`mcpNonNegativeInt` at the boundary and one past it, rather than asserting a constant against itself, and it checks the stricter per-tool ceilings against the very constants their downstream validator uses. A new integer argument that forgets its ceiling fails here.\n- **`TestMCPToolSurfaceGolden`** pins the whole declared surface \u2014 every name, description, and schema \u2014 in `testdata/mcp_tool_surface.golden.json`. There was no golden over the full tool-definition JSON; the three existing goldens cover one tool each, chosen for their token budget. This one exists because a separate unified-MCP layer is about to wrap this surface, so a silent edit to a name or a bound propagates into consumers that never see this repo. Regenerate deliberately with `UPDATE_MCP_SURFACE_GOLDEN=1`.\n\n## Cost, measured\n\nThe declared ceiling adds 16 bytes per integer argument: **+432 bytes on the `tools/list` result, 26,074 -> 26,506 (+1.7%)**.\n\nThe three per-tool byte budgets are re-measured (747->763, 810->826, 987->1003). Their **token** counts in those comments are the earlier measurement and are **not** re-measured here \u2014 the pinned o200k_base asset is deliberately not a test dependency, so I could not recompute them honestly rather than estimate.\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...` clean, `go vet ./internal/cli` clean, `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestMCP|TestDocContract'` green (73 tests). Full suite not re-run on a loaded host.\n\nStacked on #118 (`audit/contract-mcp`), which is where the tool definitions live.", "headRefName": "audit/mcp-contract-bounds", "number": 156, "title": "fix(mcp): declare the integer ceiling the tool handlers enforce"}
{"baseRefName": "audit/mcp-contract-bounds", "body": "<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/78\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nContract audit of the MCP tool surface, pass 2 \u2014 the description half. Two tools described behaviour their handlers do not implement.\n\nBoth matter more than usual: an agent picks a tool by reading these strings, and a separate unified-MCP layer is about to wrap this surface, so a wrong description propagates into consumers that never see this repo.\n\n## 1. `brain_refresh` promised a worktree semantic index it never builds\n\nThe description said, flatly: *\"Includes current worktree content by default.\"*\n\nThe `worktree` argument only ever reaches seed and docs. The handler leaves `semanticWorktree` false (`refresh.go:327` reads it, `mcp.go` never sets it), so the semantic index that `semantic=true` rebuilds is built from **committed HEAD**. The refresh command's own `RunE` carries the comment explaining why: *\"--worktree belongs to seed/docs. A dirty semantic snapshot must be an explicit `brain index --worktree` operation.\"*\n\nSo an agent calling `brain_refresh(worktree=true, semantic=true)` \u2014 the exact combination the description discusses \u2014 was left believing its semantic index reflected uncommitted work. It did not.\n\nThis is the same drift that shipped in the README as `refresh --worktree` and was corrected in #141. The MCP tool string and the two docs paragraphs describing `brain_refresh` were not part of that fix; they are corrected here.\n\n## 2. `brain_index_repository` claimed a refresh it refuses, and hid its gate\n\n- **\"Build or refresh\"** \u2014 `runSemanticIndex` errors with `semantic index already exists; use --force to replace it` unless `force=true`. The default cannot refresh. (#118 flagged this and left it, on the grounds that the `force` argument's own text disambiguates. I disagree: the top-level description is what an agent reads to *choose* the tool, and \"refresh\" is the wrong verb at exactly that moment.)\n- **Path scoping was undeclared.** The path is confined to the bound repository root unless `ENTIRE_BRAIN_MCP_ALLOW_ANY_PATH` is set, and a relative path resolves against that root rather than the working directory. This was the one path-scoped tool that did not name its gate inline \u2014 `brain_delete_project`, `brain_list_projects` and the workspace tools all do, after #133, so an agent that hits the refusal learns the knob from the surface it is already reading. It is documented in README and `docs/limits.md`, but not where the agent is looking.\n- **`profile` asserted a closed list.** It claimed *\"full, fast, or syntax-only\"*. Nothing in this repo validates the value or knows the set \u2014 it is forwarded verbatim to the external provider subprocess, and the CLI flag help for the same field says *\"e.g. full, syntax-only\"*. I could not verify whether `fast` exists, so rather than pick a side I removed the authoritative framing and made the string say what the code actually does. Flagging the two help strings still disagreeing as a follow-up for whoever owns the provider contract.\n\n## Tests that pin declaration to behaviour\n\n`mcpRefreshOptions` is extracted from the `brain_refresh` case block with **no behaviour change**, purely so the claim can be pinned against the handler rather than against a comment.\n\n- **`TestMCPRefreshWorktreeContractMatchesHandler`** asserts in one test that `worktree` reaches `seed.worktree`, that `semanticWorktree` stays false in the `worktree=true, semantic=true` combination, that the default really is `worktree=true`, and that the description says all of this. If `brain_refresh` ever does start indexing the worktree, the test fails and forces the description to move with it.\n- **`TestMCPIndexRepositoryDescribesForceAndPathScope`** drives `mcpResolveIndexPath` \u2014 absolute path contained, relative path joined onto the bound root, env gate lifting containment \u2014 beside the strings that describe it, and fails if the description drifts back to \"Build or refresh\" or drops the gate name.\n\n## Verification\n\n`go build ./...` clean, `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestMCP|TestDocContract'` green (73 tests). Full suite not re-run on a loaded host.\n\nStacked on #156.", "headRefName": "audit/mcp-contract-descriptions", "number": 157, "title": "fix(mcp): correct tool descriptions that overstate what the handler does"}
"}

